[[:db/retract 17592186083911 :question/answer-fields 17592186082397] {:db/id 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-62968591-e72a-5306-bd3f-0e762f4b0368"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-62968591-e72a-5306-bd3f-0e762f4b0368" :question/id #uuid "62968591-e72a-5306-bd3f-0e762f4b0368" :question/math-academy-id "q-353352" :question/problem "![](images/03/0353d9a2b8c8e46552e0816658628f67b58c0c6bef51bd05261becac42a55798.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,-2<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{t+2})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{0}(0+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1)(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d9da3b51-53f9-58fc-b00c-db0ea43214f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d9da3b51-53f9-58fc-b00c-db0ea43214f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9da3b51-53f9-58fc-b00c-db0ea43214f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/id #uuid "c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084390 :tutorial/content "A curve or function is defined parametrically if $x$ and $y$ are given in terms of a third parameter $t.$ This can often give rise to some interesting shapes. \n\nFor example, we can define a curve as \n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nIf we plot the curve, it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b3/b3d085ad763c368d7fafaca878c364fc13a0c83ca73b2fd17a2f349cb402a16e.png)\n\nIf we're given a parametric curve, how do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}?$ The answer is to use the chain rule. \n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange this formula into a nicer version, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{1}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use the above formula to calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for the given parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-3, \\qquad y'(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the curve is defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ the derivative is also defined in terms of $t.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083472 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084147 :step/math-academy-id 644784 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084148 :step/math-academy-id 644785 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084152 :step/math-academy-id 644788 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
